rd.\n\n**Direction 1 — "The Consultant\'s Study"** (editorial, heritage-clinical)\n- Thesis: Position the practice as a private consultant\'s study — old-Soho editorial gravitas meets modern clinical precision.\n- Adje
ctives: Considered, Discreet, Authoritative\n- Typography: high-contrast serif display (e.g., Fraunces or Libre Caslon) + neutral grotesque (Inter); ink/ivory/paper palette with deep sage; hairline rules, numbered sect
ions like a medical journal\n- Image direction: duotone sage/ink treatment of published photos, generous white space, framed like journal plates\n- Signature idea: numbered "chapters" (01 Care, 02 Services…) with hairl
ine rules; pull-quote reviews set as marginalia\n- Risk: could feel too literary/cold if serif overused; must stay warm\n\n**Direction 2 — "Uptown Clinical"** (modern Swiss-medical)\n- Thesis: A precise, grid-driven cl
inical system that reads like a world-class hospital service line — efficiency, data, and access.\n- Adjectives: Precise, Efficient, Reassuring\n- Typography: single grotesque family with wide weight range (e.g., Inter
/Neue Haas), tabular numerals for stats, uppercase micro-labels; palette: cool white, charcoal, desaturated sage as functional accent, high-contrast blue-free\n- Image direction: full-bleed color photography in strict 
grid cells, minimal treatment\n- Signature idea: a persistent "access bar" (hours/phone/book) + stat blocks (20+ years, 5.0 rating, 2 locations); cards with dense, scannable metadata\n- Risk: can feel corporate/generic
hospital; less emotional warmth\n\n**Direction 3 — "The Gentleman\'s Lounge"** (warm hospitality)\n- Thesis: Treat the visit like a members\' club appointment — warm, tactile, hospitality-led, privacy-first.\n- Adjecti
ves: Warm, Tactile, Personal\n- Typography: soft serif (e.g., Newsreader/Lora) + humanist sans; palette: warm ivory, oxblood/espresso accents with sage secondary; rounded forms\n- Image direction: warm-toned crops, rou
nded corners, collage-like compositions\n- Signature idea: "concierge" booking flow — appointment as a hosted experience; testimonial "guest book"\n- Risk: could tip into spa/lifestyle cliché and undersell surgical cre
dibility\n\nThese three differ in concept (editorial journal vs clinical system vs hospitality club), tone, typography (high-contrast serif+grotesque vs single grotesque vs soft serif+humanist sans), composition (chapt
ered marginalia vs swiss grid stat blocks vs rounded collage), image behavior (duotone framed plates vs full-bleed grid vs warm rounded crops), interaction (chapter navigation vs persistent access bar vs concierge flow
).\n\nSelection: Direction 1 "The Consultant\'s Study" — best fit: discretion + authority matches audience; the serif editorial character differentiates from every med-spa; keeps sage heritage; risk manageable. Hmm, bu
t let me think about which is really strongest. The audience: men 30-65 seeking confidential doctor-led care. The conversion: request appointment. Direction 1 combines credibility (journal-like authority) with discreti
on (private study). Direction 2 is safe but generic-clinical; Direction 3 is warm but risks spa cliché. Direction 1 it is.\n\nNow the Canva moodboard. Per TOOLING.md, Canva is via official remote MCP — I have the canva
